WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
I'm super excited to take a look at this&nbsp;
today it's a paper written by Alan Turing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.520
in 1950 and it's called Computing Machinery&nbsp;
and Intelligence. In it Alan Turing talks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.080
about the imitation game for the first&nbsp;
time which has come to be known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.880
Turing test. It's a famous idea in computer&nbsp;
science and you'll hear it mentioned often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.760
but until now I had never actually read the&nbsp;
original paper where it was explained by Alan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.400
Turing himself and I'm glad that I finally&nbsp;
did I think anyone interested in artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.320
intelligence would benefit from reading the&nbsp;
original paper so let's take a look at it.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.460
Turing starts off by considering&nbsp;
the question 'can machines think?'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:47.040
but figures that actually that question&nbsp;
is way too ambiguous. The words machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.500
and think are too ambiguous to define&nbsp;
and instead he comes up with a game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:58.440
the imitation game, which he thinks will be&nbsp;
equivalent to asking if machines can think.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:04.140
The next paragraph surprised me it's the&nbsp;
first mention of what this imitation game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:10.980
is but there's no robots involved yet. It is an&nbsp;
interrogator trying to tell the difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.720
a man and a woman, I don't think that's&nbsp;
the kind of game you would see included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:22.020
in a scientific paper these days. He says the&nbsp;
game is played with three people A is a man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:29.040
B a woman and C is the interrogator. The&nbsp;
interrogator stays in a room apart from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.920
the other two. The object of the game for&nbsp;
the interrogator is to determine which of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:41.520
the other two is the man and which is the woman.&nbsp;
In order to tell this difference the interrogator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.160
could apparently ask questions such as will X,&nbsp;
referring to one of the unknown participants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:54.120
please tell me the length of his or her hair?&nbsp;
This communication would ideally be written so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:59.880
that the tones of voice don't give anything away.&nbsp;
The man is working to deceive the interrogator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:07.200
by imitating a woman whereas the real woman is&nbsp;
working to help the interrogator make the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:13.500
identification. Now here is the important part&nbsp;
where the game evolves we now ask the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:21.600
what will happen when a machine takes the part&nbsp;
of participant A, the man, in this game? Will the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:28.620
interrogator decide wrongly as often when the game&nbsp;
is played like this as he does when the game is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:35.400
played between a man and a woman? These questions&nbsp;
replace our original, 'Can machines think?'

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.940
There are two ways that this can be interpreted.&nbsp;
One is the literal interpretation where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:47.520
man is replaced by a machine but nothing&nbsp;
else about the game changes meaning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.220
the machine has now taken over the man's&nbsp;
role of pretending to be a woman. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:58.680
version of the game we have a man trying to&nbsp;
convince an interrogator that he is a woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:06.360
competing against a machine that is doing the&nbsp;
same and trying to see which one can imitate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:12.000
woman better. This is a bit of an odd question&nbsp;
and throws the game into the complicated world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:19.020
of gender identity and expression but it is the&nbsp;
most literal interpretation of Turing's words.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:25.260
The benefit of interpreting the game like this&nbsp;
is that it disguises the participation of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.300
machine. The interrogator just thinks they&nbsp;
are trying to tell the difference between a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.460
man and a woman and they don't know that&nbsp;
machines are involved at all so they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:41.100
less biased and less on the lookout for&nbsp;
questions that would trip up a machine.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:46.980
But this isn't the standard interpretation of&nbsp;
Turing's words, the more popular understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:54.060
is that when Turing says that participant a is&nbsp;
replaced by a machine it is assumed that the goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.060 --> 00:04:00.660
of the game also changes. It's no longer about&nbsp;
pretending to be a woman but is about pretending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:07.500
to be a human full stop. The interrogator is no&nbsp;
longer picking between male and female for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:13.860
participants, instead they are choosing between&nbsp;
machine and human. This interpretation seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:20.520
be more in line with comments that Turing later&nbsp;
makes about ways to compare humans and machines.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:26.280
Things go totally off the rails in this next&nbsp;
section where Turing is trying to define the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:31.680
requirements to enter a machine into the game.&nbsp;
He seems to be a little bit worried that a team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.680
of engineers will say they've made a machine but&nbsp;
actually just enter a human that they made into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.620
the competition and so I just have to read you&nbsp;
this next part. He says one might for instance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:50.820
insist that the team of engineers should be of&nbsp;
all one sex. This would not really be satisfactory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:57.900
for it is probably possible to rear a complete&nbsp;
individual from a single cell of the skin say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:05.220
of a man. To do so would be a feat of biological&nbsp;
technique deserving of the highest praise but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:10.800
would not be inclined to regard it as the case&nbsp;
of constructing a thinking machine. This prompts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.440
us to abandon the requirement that every kind of&nbsp;
technique should be permitted. So you're allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:23.280
to enter a machine that you make but you can't&nbsp;
clone yourself or make a baby and enter that. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.860
settles on saying that only digital computers&nbsp;
will be allowed to take part in our game.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:33.300
He says there are already a number of&nbsp;
digital computers in working order.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:39.120
The digital computers in 1950 still looked&nbsp;
nothing like the digital computers that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.980
know today but he says he's not asking&nbsp;
whether the computer is available at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:49.620
present would do well but whether there are&nbsp;
imaginable computers which would do well.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:55.680
And now we get to the real juicy part of this&nbsp;
paper Alan Turing first shares his own opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.740
on the matter he says that he believes that in&nbsp;
about 50 years time which would be about the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:09.240
2000 it would be possible to program computers to&nbsp;
play the imitation game so well that an average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:16.560
interrogator would not have a more than 70 chance&nbsp;
of making the correct identification after five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:22.200
minutes of questioning. He believes that the&nbsp;
original question, 'can machines think?' is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:28.860
too meaningless to deserve discussion but that&nbsp;
by the end of the century one will be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:34.860
speak of machines thinking without expecting to&nbsp;
be contradicted. Large language models that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:41.520
have now like GPT-3 might be the kind of thing&nbsp;
that Alan Turing was trying to imagine would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:48.120
succeed at his imitation game and I think he did&nbsp;
a pretty good job of estimating what it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:55.080
able to do but his second point that by around&nbsp;
the year 2000 people would consider machines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:01.740
thinking I don't think has come true yet. ChatGPT&nbsp;
might do well at the imitation game but I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:07.080
agree with Turing's point that that would mean&nbsp;
that humans would then consider it thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:13.020
in fact what we've really seen is that we&nbsp;
continuously move the goal posts for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:19.740
we consider a thinking machine to be able to&nbsp;
do. I don't think that for us now being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:26.340
to mimic human speech in an interrogation would&nbsp;
be enough. My guess is that we don't ever want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:32.760
to consider machines thinking and so as they&nbsp;
approach any arbitrary test that we put before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:39.240
them we'll come up with a new one that is more&nbsp;
difficult and that we consider to be more human.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.880
Turing doesn't really have a lot of evidence&nbsp;
that he can present to back up his claim that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:51.120
he thinks a machine will be able to do well&nbsp;
at his imitation game so instead he tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.060
imagine every possible point someone could have&nbsp;
to try and say that machines will never think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:02.940
or that machines will never do well at this&nbsp;
game. To do this Turing has to put himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.680
in the shoes of people who don't believe&nbsp;
in his work in the same way that he does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.080
and it's pretty impressive that he's able to&nbsp;
do this. He comes up with nine objections.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:19.620
The first one is okay maybe you can make an&nbsp;
impressive machine but it will never be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:26.220
think because thinking is a function of a human's&nbsp;
immortal Soul. Turing his is not so impressed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:32.340
this argument and basically says that if God&nbsp;
is Almighty he can give a soul to whoever or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:38.280
whatever he wants to he could give a soul to an&nbsp;
elephant or he could give a soul to a machine.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:44.400
Okay but what if this is all too dreadful to&nbsp;
think about and we don't want to know about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:50.160
the heads and the sand objection. In&nbsp;
a kind of cold response to this Turing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.620
says I do not think that this argument&nbsp;
is sufficiently substantial to require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:59.280
refutation, consolation would be more appropriate.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:06.420
Okay but what about the fundamental limitations&nbsp;
of mathematics? In our current understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:12.600
of mathematics there are logical statements&nbsp;
that can lead to contradictions or paradoxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:19.740
given that a digital computer will never be&nbsp;
able to do everything. That there will be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:25.260
questions to which it will either give a wrong&nbsp;
answer or fail to give an answer at all. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:30.720
machines are built upon logical systems they&nbsp;
have a disability to which the human intellect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:37.920
is not subject. Turing says that the superiority&nbsp;
we feel in this area is actually Just an illusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:44.220
that we too give wrong answers based&nbsp;
upon our own flawed logical systems.

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:49.740
A pretty strong objection is the argument from&nbsp;
consciousness. Now he has to be pretty clever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:56.040
to come up with counterpoints to this objection&nbsp;
because it's claiming that yeah okay a machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:01.980
might be able to write a sonnet for example&nbsp;
but until you can prove that it actually has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:07.380
understood what it's written well then that&nbsp;
doesn't count as thinking. To satisfy someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:13.080
who thinks in this way you would need to prove&nbsp;
that the machine actually did have some kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:19.920
deep understanding of what it was able to produce.&nbsp;
This is a problem for humans too this is why if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.860
you write a thesis you might be asked to do&nbsp;
a Viva or an oral exam where a professor will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:32.040
ask you questions about your work and you have to&nbsp;
defend why you made the decisions that you did and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:38.700
prove by answering questions that you understand&nbsp;
your work. Turing writes up one such exam for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:43.800
machine and to be honest this looks really&nbsp;
similar to the kind of dialogue and defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:50.520
I was able to get out of ChatGPT when I asked it&nbsp;
why it made certain word choices in a poem that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:57.240
had generated. Just like Alan Turing predicted,&nbsp;
machines can already do a pretty convincing job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:03.600
of defending their work but this still doesn't&nbsp;
necessarily mean that they can think and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:10.260
they truly understand it. Philosophically we face&nbsp;
a conundrum that none of us have any evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:16.920
anyone other than ourselves is thinking but&nbsp;
for the sake of our own individual sanity we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:22.860
have to assume that other people in the world&nbsp;
can think without us being able to enter their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:28.920
mind and prove it. Any questions or debate around&nbsp;
consciousness are pretty difficult because there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:34.620
is still a lot of mystery to it but having done a&nbsp;
pretty decent job at giving some counter arguments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:39.660
here Turing has the confidence to go on and&nbsp;
then counteract another difficult objection.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:46.560
This one says that sure you can make a clever&nbsp;
machine but you will never be able to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:53.160
do X. I think my favorite part of this whole&nbsp;
paper is the ideas that Turing gives for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:59.460
X could be. He considers that you might never&nbsp;
be able to make a machine that can be kind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:07.380
resourceful, beautiful, friendly, have initiative,&nbsp;
have a sense of humor, tell right from wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:14.760
make mistakes, fall in love, enjoy strawberries&nbsp;
and cream, make some one fall in love with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:20.820
learn from experience, use words&nbsp;
properly, be subject of its own thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:27.180
have as much diversity of behavior as&nbsp;
a human or to do something really new.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:33.000
This is my favorite part of the paper because&nbsp;
considering each of these things in turn and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:38.520
wondering if a machine could ever be able to&nbsp;
do them is something that I find really fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:44.400
and interesting to think about. Turing says&nbsp;
it's perfectly reasonable to expect machines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:50.520
to be able to make mistakes, that even a&nbsp;
process of scientific induction can lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:55.440
to erronous results and thinking about the&nbsp;
claim that a machine could not be subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:59.760
of its own thought Turing says that&nbsp;
by observing the results of its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:05.760
behavior it could modify its own programs so&nbsp;
as to achieve some purpose more effectively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:11.760
He says these are possibilities of the&nbsp;
near future rather than utopian dreams.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:19.560
Number six is Lady Lovelace's objection. Ada&nbsp;
Lovelace lived long before Alan Turing so the part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:25.800
of her that is represented in this section is not&nbsp;
literally her responding and objecting to Turing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:31.320
rather it is Turing including something&nbsp;
that she had written in her time back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:36.120
when she was working on the Analytical&nbsp;
Engine with Charles Babbage, an awesome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:43.140
giant steam-powered mechanical computer. She&nbsp;
said of the Analytical Engine that it has no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:49.260
pretensions to originate anything, that it can&nbsp;
do whatever we know how to order it to perform.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:54.780
A way to interpret this could be to say&nbsp;
that a machine could never do anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.780 --> 00:14:01.860
really new or never take us by surprise but&nbsp;
perhaps it is very understandable that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:06.900
Ada Lovelace wrote this statement she didn't&nbsp;
have much reason to believe that this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:13.200
ever be possible. Her and Babbage were under no&nbsp;
obligation to claim all that could be claimed. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:17.700
could challenge this idea by considering&nbsp;
that there is nothing new under the sun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:23.940
Who can be certain that original work that&nbsp;
he has done was not simply the growth of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:28.980
seed planted in him by teaching or the&nbsp;
effect of following well-known general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:35.220
principles? Turing also notes that machines&nbsp;
take him by surprise with great frequency.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:40.440
Argument 7 explores the idea that you&nbsp;
could consider our brain and nervous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:46.020
system to be continuous whereas a&nbsp;
machine would be discrete and asks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:51.600
can a discrete state machine built&nbsp;
on just math ever a mimic a brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:57.060
with complex neurons. Turing thinks&nbsp;
it can come pretty close to doing so.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:03.300
Argument 8 says that thinking will look pretty&nbsp;
different for a human and a machine because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:09.600
our behavior is not based on a rigid set of&nbsp;
rules. We think that machines are governed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:16.140
laws and we are not, therefore we are better&nbsp;
than machines. We might not have rigid rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:22.560
of conduct or behavior but we do have the laws&nbsp;
of physics and our bodies are bound by those.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:30.180
In that way you could consider our bodies a kind&nbsp;
of machine and we also can't guarantee ourselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:35.880
that we don't follow some complete laws of&nbsp;
behavior because in the spirit of science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:42.300
you'll never reach a point where we could say&nbsp;
that we've searched enough and there are no laws.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:47.580
Number nine I'd never heard anyone mention&nbsp;
until I read it here in the paper and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:52.740
given plenty of space here so I'm not sure if&nbsp;
that means Alan Turing thought a lot about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:59.700
and took it seriously or what was going on but&nbsp;
number nine is the argument from extrasensory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:06.480
perception. It includes a line saying that&nbsp;
unfortunately the statistical evidence at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:12.060
least for telepathy is overwhelming. I don't&nbsp;
know exactly what he's referring to with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:18.180
but he does seem to consider that his imitation&nbsp;
game does need to take telepathy into account.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:25.440
He even says this argument is to my mind quite&nbsp;
a strong one. He suggests that to overcome this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:30.720
you could do something like putting the&nbsp;
competitors into a telepathy proof room.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:35.820
So now that Turing has finished convincing&nbsp;
everyone that machines should be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:42.060
do well at this imitation game the next big&nbsp;
question is how to make such a machine. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:48.060
section here is Turing at his visionary best,&nbsp;
it's him imagining a way to build a machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:54.840
and teach a machine all it knows to be good at&nbsp;
imitating a human. This part is interesting, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.840 --> 00:17:02.040
says that to best imitate an adult human mind you&nbsp;
should consider three things. One is the initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:08.880
state of the mind say at birth. The second is the&nbsp;
education to which it has been subjected and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:14.520
third is other experience not to be described&nbsp;
as education to which has been subjected.

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:20.340
So then he goes on to talk about these three&nbsp;
stages and how to replicate them in a machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:25.980
He says it would be easier to build a child's&nbsp;
mind and then teach it all that it needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:31.680
know rather than trying to build and start&nbsp;
with a knowledgeable adult mind. We can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:38.520
parallels here to what might be the first ideas&nbsp;
or mentioning of machine learning. Turing mentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:43.920
wanting to use punishments and rewards as&nbsp;
a learning process but that you'd have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:49.740
ensure that these are unemotional otherwise&nbsp;
the machine would get pretty demotivated if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:54.420
you're telling it no all the time. There's an&nbsp;
interesting point here that the machine you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:59.820
building should include a random element in&nbsp;
its learning. The random element might help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:05.880
it to have a process that's more analogous to&nbsp;
evolution and the random power of mutations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:10.200
and randomness is a key element&nbsp;
of modern machine learning.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:16.260
Turing says that we may hope that machines&nbsp;
will eventually compete with humans in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:21.780
purely intellectual fields and I love&nbsp;
this last line. He says we can only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:27.240
see a short distance ahead but we can&nbsp;
see plenty there that needs to be done.

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:33.960
So much of what Turing is imagining we've&nbsp;
been lucky to already witness and despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:40.920
that many of us are no closer to regarding&nbsp;
machines as thinking entities. I wonder if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:47.820
the human spirit can ever be put into a test&nbsp;
and what kind of test would satisfy us that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:52.680
a machine had taken its first steps&nbsp;
onto the continuum of intelligence.

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:57.660
Let me know in the comments what ideas&nbsp;
you have for a variant of this test or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:03.360
something else that would make you really&nbsp;
stop and reconsider what machines mean to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:09.120
you. Would you ever feel empathy for one?&nbsp;
And would you ever consider it to think?

